ass="c3">Properties</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c12"><td class="c39" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c0"><span>none</span></p></td><td class="c44" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c0"><span class="c3 c36">supplier, string (remove)</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c3 c36">language_code (remove)</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c3 c36">is_preferred_name (remove)</span></p></td></tr></table><h2 class="c34" id="h.o1p3rhsjx4st"><span class="c70">Trait</span></h2><table class="c14"><tr class="c12"><td class="c65" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c0"><span class="c3">Relationships</span></p></td><td class="c49" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c0"><span class="c3">Properties</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c12"><td class="c5" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c0"><span class="c1">predicate:Term</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c16 c8">supplier:Resource</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c1">metadata:MetaData</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c1">object_term:Term (value)</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c1">units_term:Term</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c16 c50">normal_units_term:Term</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c1">sex_term:Term</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c1">lifestage_term:Term</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c1">statistical_method_term:Term</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c1">contributor:Term</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c1">determined_by:Term</span></p><p class="c0"><span>compiler:Term</span></p><p class="c0 c47"><span class="c1"></span></p></td><td class="c42" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c0"><span class="c50">eol_pk</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span class="c16 c10">(= EOL measurement ID)</span></p><p class="c0"><span>resource_pk </span><span class="c16 c10">(= resource measurement ID)</span></p><p class="c0"><span>scientific_name</span><span class="c16 c10">&nbsp;(= scientificName from trait data source)</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c1">source</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c1">citation</span></p><p class="c0"><span>measurement </span><span class="c16 c10">(value from trait data source for numerical data)</span></p><p class="c0"><span>object_page_id</span><span class="c16 c10">&nbsp;(for associations)</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c1">literal</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c50">normal_measurement</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span class="c16 c10">(normalized numerical value)</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c50">normal_units</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c1">method</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c1">remarks</span></p><p class="c0"><span>sample_size</span></p></td></tr></table><p class="c9 c47"><span class="c1"></span></p><h2 class="c34" id="h.6b041rb2atvx"><span>MetaData</span><sup><a href="#cmnt6" id="cmnt_ref6">[f]</a></sup></h2><table class="c14"><tr class="c12"><td class="c65" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c0"><span class="c3">Relationships</span></p></td><td class="c49" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c0"><span class="c3">Properties</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c12"><td class="c5" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c0"><span class="c1">predicate:Term object_term:Term units_term:Term</span></p></td><td class="c42" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c0"><span class="c50">eol_pk</span><span><br>measurement<br>literal</span></p></td></tr></table><h2 class="c34" id="h.zd8evxdgc92v"><span class="c25 c59 c21">Term</span></h2><table class="c14"><tr class="c12"><td class="c65" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c0"><span class="c3">Relationships</span></p></td><td class="c49" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c0"><span class="c3">Properties</span></p></td></tr><tr class="c12"><td class="c5" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c0"><span class="c16 c21">parent_term:Term</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c16 c21">synonym_of:Term</span></p></td><td class="c42" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c0"><span class="c16 c21">uri</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c16 c21">name</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c16 c21">name_&lt;locale&gt;</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c21">section_ids</span><span class="c16 c10">[comma-separated]</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c21">type</span><span class="c16 c10">&nbsp;(measurement, association, value, metadata)</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c16 c21">definition, comment</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c16 c21">attribution</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c16 c21">is_hidden_from_overview </span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c16 c21">is_hidden_from_glossary</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c21">position </span><span class="c10">(still needed?)</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c16 c21">comment</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c16 c21">exclusive_to_clade</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c16 c21">incompatible_with_clade</span></p><p class="c0"><span class="c21">object_for_predicate </span><span class="c10">(still needed?)</span></p></td></tr></table><h1 class="c51" id="h.ut8jf78p1cav"><span class="c17">Old Input Files</span></h1><p class="c9"><span class="c19">required element</span><span>&nbsp;| not required, imported to database if present | </span><span class="c10">not required not imported to database</span></p><table class="c27"><tr class="c72"><td class="c38" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c9"><span class="c48 c56 c23">Trait Elements</span></p><p class="c9"><span class="c19">measurementID</span><span><br></span><span class="c10">parentMeasurementID</span><sup><a href="#cmnt7" id="cmnt_ref7">[g]</a></sup><sup><a href="#cmnt8" id="cmnt_ref8">[h]</a></sup><sup><a href="#cmnt9" id="cmnt_ref9">[i]</a></sup><span class="c10"><br></span><span class="c19">measurementType<br>measurementValue</span><span class="c1"><br>measurementUnit</span></p><p class="c9"><span class="c23">Metadata</span></p><p class="c9"><span>measurementAccuracy<br>measurementMethod<br>measurementRemarks<br>statisticalMethod<br></span><span>sex<br>lifeStage<br>location</span></p><p class="c9"><span class="c23">Associations</span><sup><a href="#cmnt10" id="cmnt_ref10">[j]</a></sup><sup><a href="#cmnt11" id="cmnt_ref11">[k]</a></sup><sup><a href="#cmnt12" id="cmnt_ref12">[l]</a></sup></p><p class="c9"><span class="c1">associationID<br>associationType</span></p></td><td class="c38" colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p class="c9"><span class="c23">Taxonomic Elements</span></p><p class="c9"><span class="c19">taxonID</span><span>&nbsp;[eolID, link to the EOL hierarchy]</span><span class="c19"><br>scientificName</span><span>&nbsp;[from trait data source]</span><span class="c19"><br></span><span class="c10">other taxonomic data provided by the trait data source, e.g., rank, higherClassification, taxonomicStatus</span></p><p class="c9"><span class="c23">Attribution Data</span></p><p class="c9"><span class="c19">source</span><span class="c10"><br></span><span class="c1">bibliographicCitation<br>references<br>contributor<br>compiler<br>determinedBy</span></p><p class="c9"><span class="c23">References</span></p><p class="c9"><span>referenceID<br>fullReference</span></p></td></tr></table><h2 class="c35" id="h.68u2uxxcz34m"><span class="c25 c29">New TraitBank Input Files</span></h2><ul class="c7 lst-kix_hkonsjhmx12h-0 start"><li class="c9 c24 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">A resource archive might contain only the M&amp;F file = Trait file. </span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_hkonsjhmx12h-1 start"><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">Optional additional files include Taxon and Occurrence.</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_hkonsjhmx12h-0"><li class="c9 c24 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">Only the Trait file is harvested into the graph</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_hkonsjhmx12h-1 start"><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">Data from the Taxon &amp; Occurrence files will not be accessible through graph queries and will not be included in query results. But they will be available in the resource file on Zenodo.</span></li><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">We assume that these data will not be of interest to most data users.</span></li><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">A property on the trait data record should alert data users about the availability of additional Taxon or Occurrence information in the resource file on Zenodo.</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_hkonsjhmx12h-2 start"><li class="c9 c55 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">This will make this information available to the few data users who may want to drill deeper into the source data.</span></li><li class="c9 c55 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">In the graph, this would be a resource level property that propagates to traits</span></li></ul><h3 class="c35" id="h.6usifw8ska3u"><span class="c25 c15 c23">Trait File<br></span></h3><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6qn1dmmb0hrm-0 start"><li class="c22 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">The Trait file contains all data destined for the graph and possibly additional columns for a human downloader to read.</span></li><li class="c22 li-bullet-0"><span>Trait files may have a </span><span class="c11">taxonID</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;column</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6qn1dmmb0hrm-1 start"><li class="c30 li-bullet-0"><span>If the source is a database that provides taxon identifiers, we should use these identifiers as the </span><span class="c11">taxonID</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;value</span></li><li class="c30 li-bullet-0"><span>For most resources, the </span><span class="c11">taxonID</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;will be the scientific name string used to identify the taxon in the source work. The name string can include author information or it can be a canonical. Whatever is provided by the source.</span></li><li class="c30 li-bullet-0"><span>The main function of the </span><span class="c11">taxonID</span><span>&nbsp;column is to link the trait record to a record in the </span><span class="c11">Taxon</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;file</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6qn1dmmb0hrm-2 start"><li class="c53 c55 li-bullet-0"><span>If there is no taxon file associated with a resource, we don&rsquo;t need the </span><span class="c11">taxonID</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;column.</span></li><li class="c53 c55 li-bullet-0"><span>Should we harvest </span><span class="c11">taxonID</span><span>&nbsp;values into the graph? They would be useful if they are real identifiers. If they are just name strings, they are redundant with </span><span class="c11">scientificName</span><span class="c1">.</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6qn1dmmb0hrm-0"><li class="c22 li-bullet-0"><span>Trait files will have a </span><span class="c11">scientificName</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;column. This preserves the name attached to the trait record in the source work.</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6qn1dmmb0hrm-1 start"><li class="c30 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">This may be the canonical name or the full scientific name with author data, whatever is easier to grab/mine from the resource.</span></li><li class="c30 li-bullet-0"><span class="c11">scientificName</span><span>&nbsp;values are harvested into the graph and included in trait data products.</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6qn1dmmb0hrm-0"><li class="c22 li-bullet-0"><span>Trait files will have an </span><span class="c11">tbTaxonID</span><span>&nbsp;column that links to </span><span class="c11">taxonID</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;values in the TraitBank management hierarchy whenever possible.</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6qn1dmmb0hrm-1 start"><li class="c30 li-bullet-0"><span class="c11">tbTaxonID</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;values that are simple integers link to the management hierarchy. Records with these values are harvested into the graph.</span></li><li class="c30 li-bullet-0"><span class="c11">tbTaxonID</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;values that are not simple integers (i.e., that are blank or have other strings) represent unmapped nodes. Records with these values are not harvested into the graph.</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6qn1dmmb0hrm-2 start"><li class="c53 c55 li-bullet-0"><span>Records for unmapped nodes are in the </span><span class="c11">Trait</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;file in order to represent all trait information from the source.</span></li><li class="c53 c55 li-bullet-0"><span>Unmapped nodes may become mappable with additional research.</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6qn1dmmb0hrm-0"><li class="c22 li-bullet-0"><span>Trait files may have an </span><span class="c11">occurrenceID</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;column if there is additional information in an external Occurrences file, but commonly used occurrence columns, like life stage or sex can also be included in the Trait file.</span></li><li class="c22 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">Trait files have a source column with the identifier (doi, url, handle) of the source work. Bibliographic citations will be maintained in a References masterfile. Most will be imported from CrossRef or WikiData, so we won&rsquo;t need to add those in the resource file anymore.</span></li></ul><h3 class="c35" id="h.ibejuya4eots"><span class="c25 c15 c23">Taxon File</span></h3><p class="c47 c53"><span class="c1"></span></p><ul class="c7 lst-kix_v00vw1h32eh1-0 start"><li class="c22 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">If the source provides parent child relationships, authority info or anything else that may be considered of possible interest to data users, these data can be provided in the taxon file for a human downloader, but this information will not be represented in the graph.</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_v00vw1h32eh1-1 start"><li class="c30 li-bullet-0"><span>Not all </span><span class="c11">Trait</span><span>&nbsp;files need a </span><span class="c11">Taxon</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;file</span></li><li class="c30 li-bullet-0"><span>If there is a </span><span class="c11">Taxon</span><span>&nbsp;file, </span><span class="c11">eolID </span><span>values in the </span><span class="c11">Taxon</span><span>&nbsp;file take priority over </span><span class="c11">eolID</span><span>&nbsp;values in the </span><span class="c11">Trait</span><span>&nbsp;file, i.e., we need a script to automatically load </span><span class="c11">eolID</span><span>&nbsp;from the </span><span class="c11">Taxon</span><span>&nbsp;file into the </span><span class="c11">Trait</span><span>&nbsp;file.</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_v00vw1h32eh1-0"><li class="c22 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">Data in the Taxon file should reflect names and relationships as represented in the source work. </span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_v00vw1h32eh1-1 start"><li class="c30 li-bullet-0"><span>The </span><span class="c11">taxonID </span><span>links to the </span><span class="c11">Trait</span><span>&nbsp;file.</span></li><li class="c30 li-bullet-0"><span>The </span><span class="c11">eolID</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;links to the management hierarchy</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_v00vw1h32eh1-0"><li class="c22 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">The Taxon file is not harvested into the graph.</span></li></ul><p class="c53 c47"><span class="c1"></span></p><h3 class="c35" id="h.s5m2oqha62oz"><span class="c25 c15 c23">Occurrence File</span></h3><p class="c53 c47"><span class="c1"></span></p><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6qn1dmmb0hrm-0"><li class="c22 li-bullet-0"><span>If the source provides multiple redundant trait data (</span><span class="c64 c50"><a class="c31" href="#h.9vfs4yg5zhi3">see Occurrence Data</a></span><a id="id.y8dyksfvjvb5"></a><span class="c1">), they can be included in detail here. A property on the trait record will point to this.</span></li></ul><h2 class="c34" id="h.3xwuw6bdxxs9"><span class="c18">References</span></h2><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6sk0t2rrzm61-0 start"><li class="c9 c24 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">In the new graph, we should have just one kind of object for sources &amp; references. </span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6sk0t2rrzm61-1 start"><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span>What should we call this object? </span><span class="c64 c50"><a class="c31" href="https://www.google.com/url?q=https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11826511&amp;sa=D&amp;source=editors&amp;ust=1789506313636067&amp;usg=AOvVaw1Fn14nr5FMhvl7b24EWxFH">https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11826511</a></span><span class="c1">&nbsp;(scientific work)</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6sk0t2rrzm61-0"><li class="c9 c24 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">This object will have two properties:</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6sk0t2rrzm61-1 start"><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">identifier, i.e., doi, handle, wikidata uri for things that don&rsquo;t already have an identifier</span></li><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">citation for the bibliographic citation - &gt; imported from CrossRef or WikiData</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6sk0t2rrzm61-0"><li class="c9 c24 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">Scientific Work objects will be linked to trait data records through two alternative relationships:</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6sk0t2rrzm61-1 start"><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c64 c50"><a class="c31" href="https://www.google.com/url?q=http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/source&amp;sa=D&amp;source=editors&amp;ust=1789506313636727&amp;usg=AOvVaw0MwIE3vkL0CgyoZNEoDQoY">http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/source</a></span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span></li><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c50 c64"><a class="c31" href="https://www.google.com/url?q=http://purl.org/dc/terms/references&amp;sa=D&amp;source=editors&amp;ust=1789506313636921&amp;usg=AOvVaw35RJ_0mzECKKrTWpcMtl5E">http://purl.org/dc/terms/references</a></span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6sk0t2rrzm61-0"><li class="c9 c24 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">All Scientific Work objects will be managed in one master References file with two columns: identifier, citation</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6sk0t2rrzm61-1 start"><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">When the data are harvested, the validation step for trait files should include a step where it checks if all source/reference IDs are covered in the References master file. If not, they should be added, and ideally, the citation should be resolved automagically from CrossRef, WikiData and other resources.</span></li><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">Some identifiers may not be able to be resolved to a citation and will have to be added manually by Jen or Katja. For this, we should have a simple interface, where we get a list of rows in the References master file where the citation is empty, along with a field where we can add the missing citations and send them to be added to the file.</span></li><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">For references that don&rsquo;t have any identifiers, we will make up globally unique identifiers, e.g., by using a hash. </span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6sk0t2rrzm61-2 start"><li class="c9 c55 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">These identifiers along with their citation values will be stashed in a document somewhere. </span></li><li class="c9 c55 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">The validation process for the references will check this document to fill in missing citations.</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6sk0t2rrzm61-1"><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span>The interfaces for missing citations and manually generated identifiers can probably be combined in a single document where the validation process picks up new references and deletes the ones that it has processed and drops off identifiers in need of a manually created citation.<br></span></li></ul><p class="c9"><span class="c1">Going forward:</span></p><ul class="c7 lst-kix_cgu2u9yk0aqo-0 start"><li class="c9 c24 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">Use DOIs whenever possible for source &amp; referenceID.</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_cgu2u9yk0aqo-1 start"><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">Reference validation process will resolve DOIs and add citations to the References master file.</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_cgu2u9yk0aqo-0"><li class="c9 c24 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">If no globally unique identifier is available:</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_cgu2u9yk0aqo-1 start"><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">create a WikiData record for the reference, which will be resolved by reference validation process OR </span></li><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">create an identifier (i.e., hash) and add the reference &amp; citation to the references-without-identifier list.</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_cgu2u9yk0aqo-0"><li class="c9 c24 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">If there is another globally unique identifier like a handle available, use that identifier. If it doesn&rsquo;t resolve to a citation, add the citation once it shows up in the references-without-citation list.</span></li></ul><h1 class="c51" id="h.f65tpz9xw9n9"><span class="c17">Data Products</span></h1><p class="c9"><span class="c13">taxonID</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span class="c10">[eolID = pageID]<br></span><span class="c13">scientificNameID </span><span class="c10">[link to the external reference hierarchy]</span><span><br></span><span class="c13">higherClassification</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span class="c10">[according to the EOL hierarchy]</span><span><br>scientificName </span><span class="c10">[according to the trait data source]</span><span><br></span><span class="c13">scientificName</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span class="c10">[according to the EOL hierarchy]</span><span><br>measurementType<br>measurementTypeURI<br>measurementValue<br>measurementValueURI<br>measurementUnit<br>measureme</span><span>ntUnitURI</span><span><br>measurementRemarks<br>measurementMethod<br>statisticalMethod<br>targetTaxon<br>targetTaxonSourceName<br></span><span class="c13">targetTaxonName</span><span><br>sex<br>lifeStage<br>source&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br>bibliographicCitation<br>referenceID<br></span><span class="c8">resourceURL</span><hr style="page-break-before:always;display:none;"></p><h1 class="c51" id="h.n1rrkifxc079"><span class="c17">Notes on Taxonomic Requirements</span></h1><h3 class="c35" id="h.xu7jxc2pu36"><span class="c25 c15 c23">Accepted Names in the Graph</span></h3><p class="c9"><span class="c40">We currently have two kinds of names in the graph: the preferred canonicalName for the mapped eolID and the scientificName represented in the trait data file. I think it may be a good idea to use the EOL scientificName rather than the canonical in the graph. This would make it easier to distinguish homonyms, but it may make querying more complicated because people usually search using canonical strings, not full scientificNames. Maybe we could have both in the graph, if that doesn&rsquo;t create too much overhead? Consult with Jorrit.</span></p><h3 class="c35" id="h.ndop4uo8kol3"><span class="c25 c15 c23">Synonyms in the Graph</span></h3><p class="c9"><span class="c40">Do we want to have synonyms in the graph? They could be useful if we want to let people search on them.</span></p><p class="c9"><span class="c40">If we want synonyms, how should we treat them in the data model? Treating them as properties would be simpler, but then we couldn&#39;t have their scientificNameIDs and other metadata in the graph. Is it enough if this information is available in the EOL hierarchy file?</span></p><p class="c9"><span class="c19 c41">Current thoughts</span><span class="c40">: We should treat synonyms as nodes in the graph, and we should allow traits to be attached to synonyms in the Traits file. In the graph, the trait data would then be attached to the acceptedName via its synonym. This would automatically transfer the trait mapping to the new acceptedName when the hierarchy is updated. We currently have many traits where the source uses a synonym, and we have manually mapped it to the current accepted name. We could gradually clean those up, but there will probably always be some source names that we will not want to map as synonyms, e.g., gross misspellings. </span></p><h3 class="c35" id="h.zahu9hjtxx5o"><span class="c25 c15 c23">HigherClassification Data in the Graph</span></h3><p class="c9"><span class="c40">Should we store higherClassification ID strings in addition to parent taxa in the graph? This would make the graph a lot larger, but it may make queries more efficient. Consult with Jorrit. </span></p><h3 class="c35" id="h.f2nl3oto64mv"><span class="c25 c15 c23">Legacy Taxa</span></h3><p class="c9"><span class="c40">There will be taxa in our trait data sets that cannot be cleanly mapped to any accepted taxa, because there are no synonym mappings available and/or mapping would require a deep understanding of the taxon concept used in the trait data source. </span></p><p class="c9"><span class="c40">For species it&rsquo;s probably ok to tentatively place the name in the EOL hierarchy, even if we know it&rsquo;s not in current use. This preserves the trait data record, and we can always change the mapping later if we can figure it out. </span></p><p class="c9"><span class="c40">But this is a real problem for inferred traits from older sources, e.g., the genera from the Kubitzki data set. How should we handle genera and other higher taxa if we know the trait data set name is not in use anymore or its concept has changed significantly? I don&rsquo;t think we should account for this situation in the data model, but we should talk about best practices. For example, we may decide to include data for such taxa as individual data points, but not use them for taxonomic inference. Data like this may be useful for data users who have the knowledge to do their own mappings of taxon concepts.</span></p><h3 class="c35" id="h.t41vzk9d776y"><span class="c25 c15 c23">Duplicate Taxa in Catalogue of Life</span></h3><p class="c9"><span>There&rsquo;s a lot of taxon duplication in Catalogue of Life. There are things like unrecognized synonyms, alternative representations from WoRMS, whole branches imported twice from different suppliers with different names at the root but overlapping genera and species. This often happens in branches with fossil taxa. I am inclined to omit these taxa from the EOL hierarchy, but if I do this, I will have to pick a winner, and I need to develop a procedure to mark the losers, so we know they have been excluded as duplicates in future updates. There are also complications with merging the descendants of partially duplicated branches. If I move some taxa from one COL ancestor to another, I am changing the reference hierarchy, which takes us back into more serious hierarchy management territory. I was trying to avoid this, but it may be necessary.</span></p><h3 class="c35" id="h.jkeapxcgl9sf"><span class="c25 c15 c23">Taxonomic Data from Trait Data Sources</span></h3><p class="c9"><span class="c40 c60">Taxonomic data provided by the trait data source could go into a separate Taxon file. I don&#39;t think these data should go into the graph, but they could be harvested from the resource file for extended data products.</span></p><p class="c9"><span class="c41 c62">Note that t</span><span class="c40">here may be scenarios where the taxonomic data provided by the trait data source conflicts with the EOL hierarchy. This will not be apparent in the resource file and may lead to confusion.</span></p><h3 class="c35" id="h.6sq389w55rn3"><span class="c25 c15 c23">Taxon Documentation in Data Products</span></h3><p class="c9"><span class="c1">For names, we want both the EOL scientificName and the scientificName from the trait data source. Higher classification (according to our management hierarchy) could be an optional field. What do we want to provide in terms of identifiers? In addition to the TraitBank taxonID (=eolID), we may want to provide the scientificNameID which is the link to the external hierarchy resource (usually COL). </span></p><p class="c9"><span>What if the trait data file has GUIDs as taxonIDs or provides their own mapping to external GUIDs outside EOL. Shouldn&rsquo;t we include those in data products? If so, we either need to add them to the trait properties.</span></p><h3 class="c35" id="h.tr92hrs1vz6"><span class="c25 c15 c23">Transparency for Data Users</span></h3><p class="c9"><span class="c1">We should include an eml file or something similar in both the resource files and the data packages that explain our taxonomic mapping rationale along with other things like the ontology mappings.</span></p><h3 class="c35" id="h.1yh6eypii0yt"><span class="c25 c15 c23">Taxonomic Data in Input Files</span></h3><p class="c9"><span class="c1">Initial scenario, now dismissed:</span></p><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6qn1dmmb0hrm-0"><li class="c22 li-bullet-0"><span class="c10">Trait files will have a </span><span class="c6">taxonID</span><span class="c10">&nbsp;(=</span><span class="c6">eolID</span><span class="c10">) column that links to </span><span class="c6">taxonID</span><span class="c16 c10">&nbsp;values in the management hierarchy whenever possible. </span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6qn1dmmb0hrm-1 start"><li class="c30 li-bullet-0"><span class="c6">taxonID</span><span class="c16 c10">&nbsp;values that are simple integers link to the management hierarchy</span></li><li class="c30 li-bullet-0"><span class="c6">taxonID</span><span class="c16 c10">&nbsp;values that are not simple integers represent unmapped nodes</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6qn1dmmb0hrm-2 start"><li class="c53 c55 li-bullet-0"><span class="c16 c10">These are in the Trait file in order to represent all trait information from the source, but they will not go into the graph</span></li><li class="c53 c55 li-bullet-0"><span class="c16 c10">Unmapped nodes may become mappable with additional research</span></li><li class="c53 c55 li-bullet-0"><span class="c10">The </span><span class="c6">taxonID</span><span class="c16 c10">&nbsp;values of unmapped nodes can be used to link to additional taxonomic information in the Taxon file.</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6qn1dmmb0hrm-0"><li class="c22 li-bullet-0"><span class="c10">Trait files will have a </span><span class="c6">scientificName</span><span class="c16 c10">&nbsp;column. This preserves the name attached to the trait record in the source work.</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6qn1dmmb0hrm-1 start"><li class="c30 li-bullet-0"><span class="c16 c10">This may be the canonical name or the full scientific name with author data, whatever is easier to grab/mine from the resource.</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6qn1dmmb0hrm-0"><li class="c22 li-bullet-0"><span class="c16 c10">Taxon file: </span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6qn1dmmb0hrm-1 start"><li class="c30 li-bullet-0"><span class="c16 c10">The taxonID links to the management hierarchy</span></li><li class="c30 li-bullet-0"><span class="c16 c10">Mapping nodes from the management hierarchy can be included to support complex taxon mappings for synonyms and other names not represented in the management hierarchy.</span></li><li class="c30 li-bullet-0"><span class="c16 c10">What if the source work provides GUIDs as taxonIDs or provides their own mapping to external GUIDs outside EOL? This will be rare.</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_6qn1dmmb0hrm-2 start"><li class="c53 c55 li-bullet-0"><span class="c16 c10">We could represent this information with additional identifier columns. </span></li><li class="c53 c55 li-bullet-0"><span class="c16 c10">Should we have this information in the graph, i.e., included in standard data products?</span></li></ul><h1 class="c51" id="h.mwiluz8qeax8"><span class="c17">Notes on Other Stuff</span></h1><h3 class="c35" id="h.j3v5ehxfi7n9"><span class="c25 c15 c23">Geographic Data</span></h3><ul class="c7 lst-kix_3g6incxibckz-0 start"><li class="c9 c24 li-bullet-0"><span>Going forward, only harvest geographic data that is expert curated, eg., from </span><span class="c64 c50"><a class="c31" href="https://www.google.com/url?q=https://www.gbif.org/dataset/4b3e4a71-704a-485c-917c-20a89944ea37&amp;sa=D&amp;source=editors&amp;ust=1789506313655999&amp;usg=AOvVaw1gkuxV2MVSQhzNlOXeZIF8">curated databases</a></span><span class="c1">, published country checklists, etc. </span></li><li class="c9 c24 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">Make sure charismatic groups like mammals &amp; birds are well covered. </span></li><li class="c9 c24 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">Then do a gap analysis to see how well we are doing.</span></li></ul><p class="c9 c47"><span class="c1"></span></p><h3 class="c35" id="h.9vfs4yg5zhi3"><span class="c15 c23 c25">&ldquo;Occurrence&rdquo; Data</span></h3><ul class="c7 lst-kix_vhh0gsgjkyzd-0 start"><li class="c9 c24 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">Do we want occurrence metadata in the graph?</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_vhh0gsgjkyzd-1 start"><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">Probably not. That would add a lot of extra columns that are only used rarely.</span></li><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">We also don&rsquo;t want to have a lot of redundant individual measurements for a given taxon.</span></li><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">Graph data should generally be summary data for the species, broken down by sex, life stage, geographic region if there are differences</span></li><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">Rather than weighing the graph down with lots of occurrence level metadata, we could keep these data in the resource file and link to it in the graph output.</span></li></ul><ul class="c7 lst-kix_vhh0gsgjkyzd-0"><li class="c9 c24 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">occurrences are &ldquo;individual measurement&rdquo; data records which usually have occurrence level metadata</span></li><li class="c9 c24 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">If there are multiple individual measurements for a species with similar metadata (lifestage, sex, location) in a given data file, we want to have a summarized version of these data in the measurements &amp; facts file, while the individual measurements and their detailed metadata stay in the occurrences file.</span></li><li class="c9 c24 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">The occurrences file is included in the main resource archive, and is available redundantly as a separate file in the same zenodo dataset.</span></li><li class="c9 c24 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">The occurrences file includes a column called &ldquo;summarized by&rdquo; or something, containing the measurement ID of the summary record in the M&amp;F file.</span></li><li class="c9 c24 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">The information from the occurrences file does not go into the graph, but the record with the summarized version will have a property, populated from a column in the M&amp;F file, called &ldquo;additional metadata&rdquo; or something, with a term definition explaining the relationship, and a value of [URL pointing to the occurrences file].</span></li></ul><h3 class="c35" id="h.tkbozk6ny5w5"><span class="c25 c15 c23">List of tasks for Eli&rsquo;s validation script</span></h3><p class="c9"><span class="c1">When a trait data file is ready for publication, we should run a validation script on it to make sure there aren&rsquo;t any loose ends:</span></p><ol class="c7 lst-kix_4b8attvvjkz1-0 start" start="1"><li class="c9 c24 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">If there is a Taxon file, update the eolID values in the Trait file to match the ones in the Taxon file.</span></li><li class="c9 c24 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">Trait file validation:</span></li></ol><ol class="c7 lst-kix_4b8attvvjkz1-1 start" start="1"><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">eolID values should match taxonID values in the reference hierarchy -&gt; report values that don&rsquo;t have a match.</span></li><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">stop nodes should descend from start nodes -&gt; report start/stop node pairs that are in violation. </span></li><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">uris in the trait, value, lifestage, sex, &hellip;. columns should match uris in the terms file -&gt; report uris that don&rsquo;t have a match</span></li><li class="c2 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1">referenceIDs should have an identifier match in the References master file -&gt; automatically add missing identifiers, try to fetch bibliographic citation via CrossRef, WikiData, or another service, add identifiers that cannot be resolved to a manual reference curation doc.</span></li></ol><ol class="c7 lst-kix_4b8attvvjkz1-0" start="3"><li class="c9 c24 c47 li-bullet-0"><span class="c1"></span></li></ol><div class="c52"><p class="c0"><a href="#cmnt_ref1" id="cmnt1">[a]</a><span class="c48 c18 c23">ditch Vernacular, everything will be attached directly to eol_ids</span></p></div><div class="c52"><p class="c0"><a href="#cmnt_ref2" id="cmnt2">[b]</a><span class="c48 c18 c23">Jen thinks this may be worth preserving. Keep thinking about it.</span></p></div><div class="c52"><p class="c0"><a href="#cmnt_ref3" id="cmnt3">[c]</a><span class="c18 c23 c48">Maybe launch without it and add it later.</span></p></div><div class="c52"><p class="c0"><a href="#cmnt_ref4" id="cmnt4">[d]</a><span class="c48 c18 c23">Maybe use some kind of a chatbot widget or something to implement a vernacular name translator.</span></p></div><div class="c52"><p class="c0"><a href="#cmnt_ref5" id="cmnt5">[e]</a><span class="c48 c18 c23">+1 outsourcing is the right solution for this</span></p></div><div class="c52"><p class="c0"><a href="#cmnt_ref6" id="cmnt6">[f]</a><span class="c48 c18 c23">Do we still need this?</span></p></div><div class="c52"><p class="c0"><a href="#cmnt_ref7" id="cmnt7">[g]</a><span class="c48 c18 c23">For inferred data records, some metadata (still needed for this?). Associations? See below.</span></p></div><div class="c52"><p class="c0"><a href="#cmnt_ref8" id="cmnt8">[h]</a><span class="c48 c18 c23">Current plan: check if we still need parentMeasurement for metadata. If not get rid of it and create new data structure for inferred records and associations.</span></p></div><div class="c52"><p class="c0"><a href="#cmnt_ref9" id="cmnt9">[i]</a><span class="c48 c18 c23">We decided we don&#39;t need this column anymore. Most metadata that used to be in child records can now go into the Occurrences file.</span></p></div><div class="c52"><p class="c0"><a href="#cmnt_ref10" id="cmnt10">[j]</a><span class="c48 c18 c23">Do we want to continue modeling interactions through occurrences? Or should we develop a new data model, e.g., using child records.</span></p></div><div class="c52"><p class="c0"><a href="#cmnt_ref11" id="cmnt11">[k]</a><span class="c48 c18 c23">We want to keep a separate file for Associations, but we want to get rid of the occurrence-related data model.</span></p></div><div class="c52"><p class="c0"><a href="#cmnt_ref12" id="cmnt12">[l]</a><span class="c48 c18 c23">Idea: What if TraitBank does not host any Association data? They all remain in GloBI. But people can query both data sets from TraitBank.</span></p></div></body></html>